This plugin vendors one skill from Lauren Tan's "pstack" plugin, shipped in Cursor's
official plugin repo:

    https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/main/pstack

    pstack/skills/unslop/SKILL.md -> skills/unslop/SKILL.md

Upstream commit:

    f5bdd6826fd0a0d9cbc4347134c3a74a200b9d9d

SKILL.md is the whole skill upstream: no references/, no scripts, nothing else to
carry. The rest of pstack (agents, automations, docs) is a Cursor-oriented plugin
and is deliberately not vendored here.

The upstream repo root carries no licence file. `pstack/LICENSE` is MIT and covers
this file; it is reproduced below.

LOCAL MODIFICATIONS
-------------------

None. The vendored file is byte-for-byte upstream, including the frontmatter
description ("Must always apply").

That description promises more than the mechanism delivers. A skill description is an
advertisement the model must still act on each turn, and it reliably goes unacted on
for prose-shaped turns, which is exactly where unslop matters. Upstream now also sets
`disable-model-invocation: true`, so the model cannot reach the skill on its own at
all. Delivery is therefore a SessionStart hook (`hooks/scripts/inject-unslop.mjs`)
that reads this file at runtime, strips the frontmatter, and injects the body inside
a plugin-local scoping wrapper: full ruleset for written deliverables; jargon and
plain-speech rules (26-31 and 33) for technical explanations and design discussion in
chat; the active output style wins mechanical style conflicts (rule 13's em-dash ban,
rule 26's "harness"/"surface") in conversation, unslop wins them in deliverables.

Rule 32 (mannered prose) is deliberately NOT in the chat range. It bans aphorisms,
rhetorical fragments and figurative verbs, which is the conversational register the
active output style exists to produce; the parts of it that matter for an explanation
are already banned by that style. Rule 33 (over-compression) is in the chat range,
because dropped articles and arrow-speak hurt an explanation the same way in either
place. Both apply in full to deliverables.

The rule numbers have gaps. Upstream treats them as stable ids that other skills cite
and leaves a hole rather than renumbering when a rule is retired; rules 1, 2, 4, 6 and
21 were general-prose rules retired upstream. Do not renumber to close a gap. Scoping changes belong
in the wrapper; a rule change would go through a patch under `patches/`, following
the `plugins/grill` pattern - none exists today. The vendored file itself stays
byte-for-byte upstream.

RE-SYNCING
----------

    1. Copy pstack/skills/unslop/SKILL.md over skills/unslop/SKILL.md
    2. Update the commit SHA above

